@module sound ; The sound: the tune player (two beeper channels and drums on its own interrupt), the tunes, the effects
@about TunePlay ($F852) plays a tune with A its number: 0 the title tune, 1 the finish tune (TuneTable, 6 bytes a tune: the sequence lists of channel A, channel B and the drums). It owns the machine while it plays: its own IM 2 table (every byte $FD, the vector $FDFD = JP TuneInterrupt), a loop that toggles the speaker for two square-wave channels from the alternate registers, and the drums from the interrupt every 12 frames. TuneInterrupt calls the hook at TuneHook: MenuTick on the title (the menu runs inside the tune), TuneKeyCheck (a key stops the tune) otherwise; TrailEnd blanks the hook so the finish tune always ends by itself. A channel's stream is notes (a byte under $80: NoteTable's period) and commands ($80 + n: 0 next sequence, 1 the note's attack off, 2 the decay, 3 the length, 4 the transposition). The effects are the game's own: Tone and Click (the engine from the interrupt, Landing's thump, the bonus count), Noise (random from the ROM).
@code $F850-$FB71 ; the tune player, read as data by GameEntry's, Title's and TrailEnd's copies between $F850 and $5B00
$F850 c TunePlay0 ; LD A,0: the start of an entry point for tune 0 that no one calls (Title and TrailEnd call TunePlay)
> Known only from the code.
$F852 c TunePlay ; Play tune A: the channels' sequences, the own IM 2 table and vector, the loop until the tune or a key ends it
> TuneNumber ($FB57) = A; DI; the three sequence pointers from TuneTable; the attack/decay bytes reset; $FE00-$FF00 = $FD, I = $FE, IM 2, $FDFD = JP TuneInterrupt; the first sequence of each channel; then TuneLoop. Title (c1: 4) and TrailEnd (trail1, trails: 9) call it.
> Witness: L1 15 (c1, kb, menu, trail1, trails, warp).
$F8B5 c TuneLoop ; The two channels: count down each channel's period, toggle the speaker, read the next note or command at the end of its length
> Witness: L1 15 (c1, kb, menu, trail1, trails, warp).
$F8C4 ChannelAPtr ; LD HL,stream: channel A's stream pointer (the operand patched)
$F902 ChannelAOn ; LD A,$18: the speaker byte that starts channel A's half-wave (its operand the attack's OR, patched by Envelope)
$F90B ChannelAOff ; LD A,0: the byte that ends it (the decay's)
$F913 ChannelBPtr ; LD HL,stream: channel B's stream pointer
$F951 ChannelBOn ; LD A,$18: channel B's start byte
$F95A ChannelBOff ; LD A,0: channel B's end byte
$F876 DrumBorder ; LD A,0: the border bits the drums send (the operand read by DrumNoise and DrumHit)
$F961 c TuneInterrupt ; The tune's IM 2 handler: count the channels' note timers, the drums every 12 frames, the hook
> Witness: L1 6910 (c1, empty, fuel, kb, menu, trail1, trails, warp).
$F96C TuneHook ; CALL MenuTick or TuneKeyCheck (patched by MenuLoop and TrailEnd; three zeros for none)
$F977 c TuneKeyCheck ; Any key: stop the tune
> Witness: L1 5388 (c1, empty, fuel, kb, menu, trail1, trails, warp).
